---
import Article from '../layouts/Article.astro';
import FeeTable from '../components/FeeTable.astro';
import Plate from '../components/Plate.astro';

const entryRows = [
  {
    service: 'Pre-Construction Audit',
    structure: 'Fixed scope: budget, contract, schedule and team, reviewed before ground breaks.',
    fee: 'Flat $25,000–60,000, set by project scale and existing documentation',
    href: '/services/pre-construction-audit',
  },
  {
    service: 'Project Recovery / advisory',
    structure: 'Piecemeal and dispute work, scoped in writing before it starts.',
    fee: '$300–450 per hour',
    href: '/services/project-recovery',
  },
  {
    service: 'Holdback & Draw Compliance',
    structure:
      'Statutory holdback administration with every draw verified before release. Scales with draw frequency.',
    fee: 'Monthly, quoted with the engagement',
    href: '/services/holdback-and-draw-compliance',
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'How is this different from what my general contractor’s project manager does?',
    a: 'The activities overlap; the obligation doesn’t. The GC’s project manager is employed by the contractor, and when your interest and the contractor’s diverge — an ambiguous scope item, a change order, a delay — their duty runs to their employer. We do much of the same reading and tracking, on the other side of the table, paid by you alone.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need this if I trust my builder?',
    a: 'Trust is necessary and not sufficient. Public companies audit their books without accusing their accountants. On a multi-year project with dozens of firms and thousands of transactions, honest errors alone justify independent review — and the best builders tend to welcome it, because verified draws get paid without argument.',
  },
  {
    q: 'When should we engage someone?',
    a: 'Before the construction contract is signed, ideally. Most of the leverage in this work sits in the contract, the bid comparison and the first payment applications. Engaging mid-construction still helps; it just costs more and achieves less than the same attention would have at the start.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does an owner’s representative typically cost?',
    a: 'Across the industry: one to five percent of construction cost, with the percentage falling as project size rises; monthly retainers commonly run five to twenty-five thousand dollars; hourly advisory work runs roughly $150–450 depending on the firm. Our own published fees are above, and full representation is quoted per engagement after we understand the project.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do you charge for the first conversation?',
    a: 'No.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will you tell us if we don’t need you?',
    a: 'Yes, and it has happened. Some projects are well served by their existing team, and saying so costs a fee and earns a referral.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<Article
  title="Fees — How We’re Compensated"
  heading="How we’re compensated"
  kicker="Fees"
  description="How an owner’s representative is compensated: fixed-fee pre-construction audits, percentage or retainer for full engagements, and what we refuse to accept."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <p>
    Two things belong on this page: what the fixed-scope work costs, and how we think about the rest. Both
    are here because a firm selling independence should be legible about its own money.
  </p>

  <h2>Fixed-scope engagements — published</h2>
  <div class="not-prose mt-6">
    <FeeTable caption="Quoted before applicable taxes. Travel and disbursements at cost." rows={entryRows} />
  </div>

  <Plate
    src="/plates/plate-07-rule.svg"
    alt="A steel measuring rule on an empty field, casting one shadow"
    caption="Fixed scope, fixed fee, stated before work begins."
    class="my-12 aspect-[21/9]"
  />

  <h2>Full representation — how it’s structured</h2>
  <p>Priced two ways, and which one suits a project depends on how defined the scope is:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A percentage of construction cost</strong>, billed monthly with a final reconciliation at
      completion. The percentage steps <em>down</em> as project size rises — a larger project takes more
      hours but not proportionally more, and a flat percentage would make it subsidise smaller work.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A monthly retainer</strong>, which suits open-ended or extended scopes better.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    We quote it after we understand the project, not before. Anyone who quotes a percentage before seeing the
    drawings and the contract is guessing.
  </p>

  <h2>What we will not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No percentage of savings.</strong> It sounds aligned and isn’t — it creates an incentive to cut
      scope and quality rather than protect the project.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No compensation from any party but the client.</strong> No referral fees, rebates or
      commissions from builders, subcontractors, suppliers, designers or lenders.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No contingency arrangements</strong> tied to project outcomes.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why start with the audit</h2>
  <p>
    Most of the value in this work is delivered before anything is built. The
    <a href="/services/pre-construction-audit">audit</a> is the cheapest way to find out whether you want us
    for the rest — and it is a useful document even if you never engage us again.
  </p>

  <h2>Common questions</h2>
  <div class="not-prose mt-6 max-w-prose">
    {
      faqs.map((f) => (
        <details class="group border-t border-rule py-4">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer text-base font-semibold marker:text-accent">{f.q}</summary>
          <p class="mt-3 text-[0.95rem] leading-relaxed text-ink/90">{f.a}</p>
        </details>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <p class="mt-10">
    <a href="/project-review">Request a project review</a> — the reply is a scoped proposal with the fee
    stated and the deliverables named.
  </p>
</Article>
